// Compile-time build outputs (proposal/const-eval.md §3): `emit(kind, line)`
// appends a line to the build's `kind` asset. The channel deduplicates by
// line, orders deterministically, and writes `<output>.<kind>` beside the
// compiled JS. Compile-time-only: callable only from within a `const`
// expression — a runtime call path is a compile error.

[extern("__emit_asset")]
external fun emit(kind: str, line: str): void;

// Compile-time build inputs (proposal/docs-port.md §3.3, markdown.md §7 —
// the read direction of the channel above): `read(path)` returns the file's
// text. The path must be relative and resolves against the package root (the
// same base imports resolve under), never the process working directory; a
// path that is absolute, escapes the root, or names no readable file is a
// compile error at the `const` expression. Every file read becomes a tracked
// build input: a change to it invalidates the compile that read it.
// Compile-time-only, exactly like `emit`.

[extern("__read_asset")]
external fun read(path: str): str;

// Compile-time build outputs, whole FILES (kolt.local 029 — `emit`'s sibling
// on the other axis: `emit` accumulates LINES into one generated file, this
// carries an existing file through unchanged). `bundle(path)` tells the build
// to copy the file into the output directory beside the compiled JS, and
// returns the url its copy answers on: `bundle("static/logo.png")` writes
// `dist/static/logo.png` and evaluates to `"/static/logo.png"`.
//
// The path is the name. It is relative and resolves against the package root
// (the same base imports and `read` resolve under), it is `/`-separated on
// every host, and the copy keeps it — so a subdirectory survives, two
// different files can never claim one output name, and nothing is renamed
// behind your back. Where a resource lands in `dist/` is therefore decided by
// where you put the file. A path that is absolute, escapes the root, or names
// no readable file is a compile error at the `const` expression.
//
// The file becomes a tracked build input exactly as `read`'s does: change it
// and the compile that named it is invalidated, so `run --watch` recopies it.
// Reachability stays the compiler's — a file no `const` names is not copied,
// so it does not ship.
//
// A browser leg's build manifest lists what it bundled, which is what makes
// `serve_build` serve these with no route of your own
// (`std::http::ServerBuilder::serve_build`).
//
// Compile-time-only, exactly like `emit` and `read`.

[extern("__bundle_asset")]
external fun bundle(path: str): str;
